<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> Chronicle is a collection of annals in Old English recording the history of the English from the Roman period to the twelfth century. First compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in the late ninth century, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, it was copied and continued in several monasteries, and the last entries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> version reach 1154. It is the main narrative source for Wessex and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle.jpg" alt="Anglo-Saxon Chronicle" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographed by Geogre, original author, monastic scribes in Peterborough · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peterborough.Chronicle.firstpage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first version of the Chronicle is lost. Known as the Common Stock, it was created in the late ninth century, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> (r. 871–899), and dates to around 893, since it covers the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> from the Continent in that year and a version was available to Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> for his Life of King Alfred of 893. It drew on annals relating to Kent, the South Saxons, Mercia and, above all, Wessex, and on sources now otherwise lost, some as early as the seventh century. Its earliest annal, for 60 BCE, records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>The Common Stock was intended chiefly to legitimise the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and the reign of Alfred. Copies were made and sent to monasteries across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where they were updated, partly independently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Almost all the material is in annals, year by year. Nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive in whole or in part, labelled A to I; none is the original. The oldest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> or Parker Chronicle, was begun at Old Minster, Winchester, towards the end of Alfred's reign and carries entries in the hands of several scribes through the tenth century. The Peterborough Chronicle was copied after a fire at that monastery in 1116 and continued to 1131, and in one manuscript the annals were still being added in 1154.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> reflect the places and aims of their compilers. One includes the Mercian Register, covering 902 to 924 with a focus on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a>. Most copies include a poem on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunanburh">Battle of Brunanburh</a> in 937. The annal for 829 says the Northumbrians offered Egbert submission and peace, whereas the Northumbrian chronicles used by Roger of Wendover say he laid waste the province and made King Eanred pay tribute. Copying also introduced errors: the whole Common Stock is out by two years for the period 756 to 845.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Much of what the Chronicle records appears nowhere else, and it provides a relatively clear chronological framework, so it is among the most influential sources for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between the end of Roman authority and the decades after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>. Nicholas Howe called it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s Ecclesiastical History of the English People the two great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> works of history. Its annals from 1131 onwards in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> text give key evidence for the change from Old English to early Middle English.</p><p>John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, William of Malmesbury and Henry of Huntingdon each had a copy and adapted it. Benjamin Thorpe's edition of 1861 gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> the letters used today, and the standard modern English translations are by Dorothy Whitelock, David Douglas and Susie Tucker, and by Michael Swanton.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Saxon Chronicle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b>, first compiled in Wessex in Alfred’s reign, was still being kept up at Peterborough Abbey in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b> recorded early English history.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b> states that in 1085 the king held a large meeting and deep consultation with his council about the land, how it was occupied and by what sort of men, and then sent his men into every shire to find out how many hides each held,…</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>King of the English since 959, Edgar was consecrated with his wife Ælfthryth on Whit Sunday; the <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b> implies it was a first coronation, though historians debate whether it was a second.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a Norse revival from York, Edmund of England recovered the Five Boroughs in 942, an event celebrated in a poem in the <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</b> dates Bernicia to 547, when Ida took the kingship and built Bamburgh.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li></ul></section>
